 pain or shortness of breath.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling.\n• Endocrine: Endorses diabetes, denies any swelling in legs.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: In no apparent distress.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n• Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly.\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left knee: Slight erythema and edema present. Small effusion present over the left knee. Decreased flexion and extension of the knee. Strength 5/5 in bilateral lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee were obtained and reviewed in office today. These are normal.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• CBC: Within normal limits.\n• A1c: Slightly elevated.\n• Lipid profile: Pending.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nJanet Brown is a 33-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of left knee pain.\n\nLeft knee pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s x-rays of the left knee were normal. The patient has been doing more stair workouts, which she thought was building muscle on her knees and was supposed to help. She denies any other joint pain such as elbows or shoulders.\n• Additional Testing: Autoimmune profile.\n• Additional Treatment: Referral to physical therapy. Anti-inflammatory medication.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure was elevated today.\n• Additional Testing: Lipid profile.\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her A1c was slightly elevated.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review her blood pressure readings and lipid profile. She will follow up with physical therapy for her left knee pain. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY 