ahead and do a quick physical exam on you . so , hey dragon , show me the blood pressure . your blood pressure\'s actually pretty high today , so you might be a little nervous here , which is not uncommon .\n[patient] yeah .\n[doctor] um , so , looking at you , your , your neck is nice and soft , there\'s no enlarged thyroid . your heart has a regular rate and rhythm . your lungs are clear . your abdomen is nice and soft , but looking at your knees , on your left knee you do have a slight erythema and edema . there is a small efusion present over your left knee . um , you have some decrease flexion and extension of your knee , but your strength in your lower extremities is good . uh , so let\'s go ahead and look at some of your results . i know that we did some x-rays when you came in .\n[patient] yeah .\n[doctor] hey dragon , show me the left knee x-ray . okay , so looking at this , this is an , this is a normal x-ray of your left knee , so you may just have a little bit of a strain going on in your , in your knees there .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] uh , let\'s look at some of your labs , \'cause i know that you have the diabetes . hey dragon , show me the labs . so , here your white blood cell count is not elevated , so i\'m not concerned about an infection or anything like that . um , hey dragon , show me the diabetes\' labs . okay and , and your a1c is a little elevated , but it\'s not , it\'s not terrible , so i think for right now we\'ll just continue you on the current regimen . um , so my plan from you , for you in terms of the joint pain , um , i\'d like to go ahead and , you know , we\'ll just send some autoimmune labs to work , work up your left knee pain and , uh , we\'ll work for you to do some physical therapy and i\'d like to go ahead and give you anti-inflammatory medication to help with the pain . does that sound okay ?\n[patient] that sounds great .\n[doctor] okay . hey dragon , order meloxicam 15 mg once a day . hey dragon , order a physical therapy referral . hey dragon , order an autoimmune profile . and then from a hypertension standpoint you , because your blood pressure is a little elevated today , i\'d like you to continue to monitor it at home and , you know , send me a report in the next couple of weeks sh- , you know , giving me the , the blood pressure reportings . do you have a blood pressure cuff at home ?\n[patient] yes , i do .\n[doctor] okay . and then , um , we\'ll go ahead and order a lipid profile for you . hey dragon , order a lipid profile . and then for , from a diabetes standpoint let\'s go ahead and , uh , continue you on your metformin 500 mg twice a day . okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] do you have any questions ?\n[patient] no , that\'s it .\n[doctor] okay . the nurse will be in soon . it was good to see you .\n[patient] nice seeing you too .\n[doctor] hey dragon , finalize the note .\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJanet Brown is a 33-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of left knee pain.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been experiencing pain in her left knee for the past 2 weeks. The pain is bilateral and is worse with activity. She notes that she has been doing more stair workouts, which she thought was building muscle on her knees and was supposed to help. She denies any other joint pain such as elbows or shoulders, but she notes that she had a rotator cuff repair 2 years ago. She denies any pins and needles in her feet or swelling in her legs. She does note that she monitors her blood sugar levels and has diabetes. She denies fever, chills, nausea, vomiting, chest pain, or shortness of breath. She has been taking Norvasc 5 mg daily and has been compliant with it. She denies that her blood pressure has elevated recently.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses bilateral knee pain.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling.\n• Endocrine: Endorses diabetes, denies any swelling in legs.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: In no apparent distress.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n• Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly.\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left knee: Slight erythema and edema present. Small effusion present over the left knee. Decreased flexion and extension of the knee. Strength 5/5 in bilateral lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee were obtained and reviewed in office today. These are normal.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• CBC: Within normal limits.\n• A1c: Slightly elevated.\n• Lipid profile: Pending.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nJanet Brown is a 33-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of left knee pain.\n\nLeft knee pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s x-rays of the left knee were normal. The patient has been doing more stair workouts, which she thought was building muscle on her knees and was supposed to help. She denies any other joint pain such as elbows or shoulders.\n• Additional Testing: Autoimmune profile.\n• Additional Treatment: Referral to physical therapy. Anti-inflammatory medication.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure was elevated today.\n• Additional Testing: Lipid profile.\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her A1c was slightly elevated.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review her blood pressure readings and lipid profile. She will follow up with physical therapy for her left knee pain. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY 