cephalus.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristopher Watson is a pleasant and cooperative 86-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. The patient has been followed by me since May of 2020 when he first presented with an 8-10 year history of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with a spino-catheter protocol in June 2020, and he underwent shunt surgery on August 1st, 2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in a ventricular-peritoneal configuration was programmed at a level 2.0 was placed.\n\nThe patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. He notes that his walking has improved. He is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance, which he was unable to do prior to surgery. However, he still has intermittent difficulties with bladder control and continues to wear a diaper. He denies any issues with sores from the diaper and any decreased or painful urination. His bladder control did improve after surgery, but has leveled out. He notes that his cognitive function has improved as well. He states that he feels less "lost" when conversing with others now. The patient denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is located. He denies any abdominal pain.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\n• Adult hydrocephalus\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\n• Patient is a retired male living in a rural area. He is a retired farmer. He enjoys watching TV and reading books. He lives with his daughter who assists him with daily activities.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\n• Daughter reports that his cognitive function has improved. She states that she feels like she can have a normal conversation with him again.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss. Reports impaired ability to carry out daily functions.\n• Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Denies any genital sores, decreased or painful output.\n• Neurological: Reports walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.\n• Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\n• Blood pressure: 124/80\n• Heart rate: 64\n• Respirations: 18\n• Weight: 174 lbs\n• Pain: 0/10\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Short PE: Normal. MMSE: 26/30 on recent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration. Fund of knowledge: 25/30 on spelling and 25/30 on calculations. Immediate and delayed recall: 2/3 memory words with cuing and 1/3 with multiple choice. This is a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling. The patient was unable to recall any memory words with cuing without and only 1 with multiple choice at that time. Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance score of 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17/28. Cranial nerves II-XII grossly intact. Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor exam shows normal bulk and strength. Coordination is slow from no-finger-to-nose. CT scan from 10/15/2020 of the head shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Adult hydrocephalus\n• Gait impairment\n• Urinary incontinence and urgency\n• Cognitive impairment\n\nPLAN\n\nI recommended that we continue to monitor the patient in the office every 3 months. I advised the patient that it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level needed to pass a driver safety evaluation at this point. He could, however, enroll in a driver safety program and continue to work towards passing the evaluation at that point. I also advised that he could continue to see me for cognitive therapy to improve his cognitive function. I recommended that he continue to wear a diaper and take sitz baths as needed to prevent sores. We will review the CT scan results today and leave the shunt setting for another 2-3 months.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up adult hydrocephalus.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Christopher Watson is a 86-year-old male who returns in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. The patient is accompanied by his daughter, Theresa Watson. The patient is being seen for the first time since undergoing shunt surgery on 08/01/2020.\n\nThe patient reports that his walking has improved since surgery. He is now able to walk to the dining room. He also states he is doing better mentally. He states he is not feeling lost when speaking with people anymore.\n\nThe patient denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is. He also denies any pain in his belly.\n\nMs. Watson interjects and states that her father was not able to walk to the dining room prior to surgery. She also states that his bladder control did improve after surgery but has leveled off and he now wears diapers. She denies any sores from wearing the diaper.\n\nMs. Watson also states that her father has been doing much better since surgery.\n\nMs. Watson interjects and states that her sister, Hannah, stated that she can now have a normal conversation with her father again.\n\nThe patient is doing well. He denies any issues.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nMr. Christopher Watson is married. He denies smoking tobacco.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\n• Medtronic Strata Shunt, programmed at a level 2.0.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Negative for fever or unintentional weight loss.\n• Genitourinary: Positive for urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Negative for genital sores, decreased or painful output.\n• Neurological: Positive for continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Negative for headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.\n• Psychiatric: Negative for depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\n• Blood pressure: 124/80.\n• Heart rate: 64.\n• Respirations: 18.\n• Weight: 174 pounds.\n• Pain: 0/10.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Constitutional: Normal\n• Gait: Slightly slow and unsteady gait without an assistive device. Tinetti assessment tool: Gait score 6 to 8/12 and balance score 12/16 for a total score of 18 to 20/28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15 to 17/28.\n• Neurological: Cranial nerves are intact. Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysmetria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from no-finger to nose.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCT scan of the head 10/15/2020 reveals a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Monro of 4.6 centimeters with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 millimeters. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right front region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well and has shown improvement since his shunt surgery. The CT scan shows a tiny amount of blood in the right parietal region, but this is not causing any symptoms.\n• Medical Treatment: I have recommended that we wait another 2 to 3 months before we change the shunt.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I have discussed the risks and benefits of continuing to wait to change the shunt. The patient is satisfied with the plan.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has shown improvement in his gait and cognitive abilities.\n• Medical Treatment: I have recommended that we continue to monitor his progress.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I have discussed the importance of following up regularly.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s urinary incontinence and urgency have not improved.\n• Medical Treatment: He will continue to wear a diaper.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I have advised him to report any sores or pain associated with wearing the diaper.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up adult hydrocephalus, 3 months status post shunt surgery.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns in follow up of adult hydrocephalus. This is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery 08/01/2020. He is accompanied by his daughter, Theresa, together they give his history.\n\nI have followed Mr. Watson since May 2020. He presented with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with the spinal catheter protocol in June 2020 and he underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventriculoperitoneal configuration programmed at level 2.0 was placed.\n\nMr. Watson has had no hospitalizations or other illnesses since I last saw him. With respect to his walking, his daughter states that he is now able to walk to the dining room just fine but could not before his surgery. His balance has improved though he still has some walking impairment. With respect to his bladder control, initially there was some improvement, but he has leveled off and he wears a diaper. With respect to his cognition, both Theresa and the patient say that his thinking has improved. The other daughter, Hannah summarized it best according to the two of them, she said “I feel like I can have a normal conversation with him again.” Mr. Watson has had no headaches and no pain at the shunt site or in the abdomen.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nUrinary incontinence.\nAdult hydrocephalus.\nGait impairment.\nCognitive impairment.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional Symptoms: Significant for impaired ability to carry out daily functions. Negative for fever or unintentional weight loss.\n• Genitourinary: Significant for urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Negative for genital sores, decreased or painful output.\n• Neurological: Significant for continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Negative for headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.\n• Psychiatric: Negative for depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure 124/80, heart rate is 64, respiratory rate is 18, weight 174 pounds, and pain is 0/10.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nMr. Watson is a pleasant and cooperative man who is able to converse easily though his daughter adds some details.\n\nHead and Face\nThe shunt site was clean, dry, and intact and confirmed at a setting of 2.0.\n\nNeurological\nMental status: Tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, concentration, and fund of knowledge. He scored 26/30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25/30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get two of the three memory words with cuing and the third one with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling and at that time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing and only one with multiple choice.\nGait: Tested using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance of score of 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. This has slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17/28.\nCranial Nerves: Pupils are equal. Extraocular movements are intact. Face symmetric. No dysarthria.\nMotor: Normal for bulk and strength.\nCoordination: Slow for finger-to-nose.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCT scan of the head from 10/15/2020: It shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Munro of 4.6 cm with a 3rd ventricular contour that is flat with the span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. This was not noticed by the radiologist who stated no extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Adult hydrocephalus.\n• Gait impairment.\n• Urinary incontinence and urgency.\n• Cognitive impairment.\n\nMr. Watson has made some improvement with his hydrocephalus since shunt surgery. There is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. I do not believe that this tiny amount of fluid is symptomatic, and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the CT scan.\n\nMr. Watson asked me about whether he will be able to drive again. Unfortunately, I think it is unlikely that his speed of movement will improve to a level that he will be able to pass a driver\'s safety evaluation, however, occasionally patients surprise me by improving enough over 9 to 12 months that they are able to pass such a test. I would certainly be happy to recommend such a test if I believe he is likely to pass it and he is always welcome to enroll in a driver\'s safety program without my recommendation, however, I think it is exceeding unlikely that he has the capability of passing this rigorous test at this time. I also think it is quite likely he will not regain sufficient speed of motion to pass such a test.\n\nPLAN\n\nI had a long discussion with the patient and his daughter. We are all pleased that he has started to make some improvement with his hydrocephalus because of the fluid collection in the right parietal region, I would like to leave the setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing the shunt.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow up in 3 months.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
