pril 20 mg PO QD. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been drinking alcohol 4-5 times per week hard alcohol. She is struggling with not drinking. She is taking her blood pressure medications. She reports taking half of the lisinopril. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness or shortness of breath. She is checking her blood pressure at home. She reports that her blood pressure is 130-145/95-100 in the morning and 145/100 in the evening. She reports that she is sitting for a while before taking her blood pressure in the evening. She is taking her medicine in the morning when she gets up. She reports that she feels better when she only takes half of the lisinopril.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nEars, Nose, Mouth and Throat\n- General Examination: Skin is moist, good turgor.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallop, or rub.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: No edema, no tenderness.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Unlabored breathing, clear to percussion and auscultation.\n\nNeurological\n- Examination: No focal deficits, no tremor, no CVA tenderness.\n\nPsychiatric\n- Examination: No anxiety, agitation, or depression.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAlcohol use disorder.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports drinking alcohol 4-5 times per week to excess.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the potential benefit of alcohol cessation for her hypertension.\n- Medical Treatment: She would like to start naltrexone. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2022. We reviewed side effects and risks.\n\nHypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: She is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlororthiazide 12.5 mg daily. Her blood pressure was 150/100 at her last visit.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue her current medications. Her target blood pressure is 130-14