s.\n\nThe patient reports that her perioral dermatitis is doing much better than before. She states that she has a small amount of residual rash. The patient notes that she started using Doxycycline 100 mg once a day because she believes the pharmacist suggested that if it is getting better, to limit it to once a day. The patient notes that she was taking it twice a day and did notice a lot of improvement. She stopped breastfeeding and asked for the Doxycycline at that time. She denies breastfeeding at this time.\n\nThe patient is currently using Sulfacetamide wash twice a day. She denies that it is drying her out. She is also using Metro cream after she washes her face. She denies using any lotions. She is not using Cetaphil cleanser or Neutrogena wipes.\n\nThe patient is not currently breastfeeding.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nSkin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV-V. Bilateral medial cheeks have a few erythematous papules. There is a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Perioral dermatitis.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing much better than before. There is a small amount of residual rash. I recommend that she continue to use Doxycycline 100 mg twice a day. I also recommend that she use Tacrolimus 0.1% ointment twice a day until the rash is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping. I will rewrite her instructions.\n• Additional Testing: None indicated.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that Doxycycline can cause gastrointestinal upset and sun sensitivity. She should use sunscreen and wear a hat when she is outside. I advised her that the Tacrolimus ointment may cause a tingling or "needles and pins" sensation that lasts for a few seconds and then goes away. It is not an allergy and it will not cause her skin to thin.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks. I will cancel the Elidel cream prescription. I will give her a few samples of Vanicream. She should not use any anti-aging or plant-based products.\n\nThe patient will continue to use Sulfacetamide wash once a day. She will continue to use Metro cream after she washes her face. She will use Tacrolimus ointment until the rash is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping. She will discontinue the use of Doxycycline after 1 week. She will not start any new products until she sees me again. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRash.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a pleasant 47-year-old female who presents for follow-up of rash. She is doing well. She reports that she has a small bit leftover. She started taking doxycycline only one a day because she thinks the pharmacist said if it\'s getting better, to just limit it to once a day. She was taking it twice a day and did notice a lot of improvement. She started doing only once a day and it seems the same and it\'s not continuing to get better. She denies breastfeeding at this time. She stopped breastfeeding and then asked for the doxycycline at that time.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline 100 mg tablet, twice a day, started 2 weeks ago.\nSulfacet wash, twice a day.\nMetro cream, after face wash.\n\nALLERGIES\n\nNone.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Ears, Nose, Mouth, and Throat: Denies ear, nose, and throat pain or discomfort.\n• Eyes: Endorses ocular rosacea.\n• Skin: Endorses rash.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Skin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV, not prone to burning, tans easily. Bilateral medial cheeks few faint erythematous papules, little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Perioral dermatitis.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is concerned about the rash. She has been taking doxycycline once a day for the past week. I recommend continuing to take the doxycycline twice a day.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained that the doxycycline can cause heartburn or stomach upset. I also explained that she should continue to use the face wash twice a day. I explained that the doxycycline can make her skin more sensitive to the sun, so she should use sunscreen.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue doxycycline 100 mg tablet twice a day. Continue to use the face wash twice a day. Apply metro cream after washing the face.\n\n2. Rosacea.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient endorses ocular rosacea.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained that rosacea can cause redness and flushing. I explained that she should continue to use the face wash twice a day. I explained that she should avoid using products that can cause irritation.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue to use the face wash twice a day.\n\n3. Dry skin.\n- Medical Reasoning: She has been using the face wash twice a day. I explained that she should continue to use the face wash once a day and apply a moisturizer.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained that the doxycycline can cause dry skin. I explained that she should use a moisturizer.\n- Medical Treatment: I gave her a sample of Vanicream. I also gave her a sample of petroleum jelly. She can use either of these for dry skin.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for perioral dermatitis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. She also has a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea. Today, she states that her perioral dermatitis is well-controlled, although it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week, she has been using the doxycycline only once per day. She has not seen continued improvement but it has not worsened. She is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


