consciousness.\n\nVITALS\n\nHis vitals are normal. He has a blood pressure of 122/76, heart rate of 82, and his oxygen saturation is 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right shoulder: Pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the AC joint. Decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Palpable radial artery pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right shoulder obtained in office today revealed an acute AC joint fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight shoulder pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we treat him conservatively. I have recommended that we place him in a sling. I have prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg once a day. I have ordered a CT scan of his right shoulder to further evaluate the fracture. I have also referred him to physical therapy to strengthen his shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nHe will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight shoulder pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he is an avid football player.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of appendicitis and appendectomy.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of appendectomy.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Tylenol.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right shoulder pain.\nNeurological: Denies headache, dizziness, or loss of consciousness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 122/76 mmHg\nHR: 82 bpm\nOxygen Saturation: 95% on room air\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n- Examination of the right shoulder: Pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the AC joint. There is decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. Pain with supination and pro