PLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Anderson is a 34-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his pain began approximately 3 days ago when he was playing a pickup soccer game. The patient reports that he felt a pop in his right knee while attempting to plant and kick the ball. Since the injury, he has noticed swelling in his right knee, which has decreased slightly. He describes the pain as being between 6-7 out of 10, and it is primarily a limited range of motion in his right knee. The patient notes that he is able to bear weight on his right leg, but it feels loose and he has difficulty with the last few degrees of flexion and extension. He has been taking anti-inflammatory medications, specifically ibuprofen, which has provided some relief.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, swelling, limited range of motion.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurovascular: Bilateral lower extremities intact. Muscle strength 5/5 in bilateral lower extremities.\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion present. Slight ecchymosis. Pain to palpation at the medial joint line. Positive Lachman exam. Unable to fully extend to 0 degrees. Limited to 115 degrees. Positive for pain with passive range of motion.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee injury, likely anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and symptoms today, I have had a lengthy discussion with him regarding his current condition. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of his right knee to evaluate the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament. Based on my examination today, I am highly suspicious that the patient has an anterior cruciate ligament tear. However, we will not make any definitive treatment decisions until we have the results of the MRI. I have recommended that he use crutches and a knee immobilizer. I have also recommended that he take meloxicam 50 