 but it has not helped. She states that the pain is keeping her up at night. She denies that her pain is actively being sought by a chainsaw. She states that the pain is a 7 out of 10.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she has a history of hypertension and diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she enjoys pottery and baking.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she takes Lisinopril 10 mg daily and metformin 500 mg twice daily. She states that she takes metformin for her diabetes, but she admits that she sometimes forgets to take it. She states that she likes to bake and make cinnamon rolls. She reports that she has been doing this since she was very young. She states that she has been doing pottery for a while. She has been doing pottery for a while. She states that she is big on pottery. She has been making a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. She states that she paints and decorates it. She has been working on it a lot and it has been hurting her. She has been doing this for a while. She has been doing this for a while.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports difficulty sleeping due to pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.\nEndocrine: Reports elevated blood sugars.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 120/70.\nPulse: 80.\nRespiratory rate: 18.\nTemperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nO2 Saturation: 98% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Alert and oriented x3.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness to palpation over the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and extension of the right wrist. Pain with pronation of the right wrist. No ecchymosis or erythema. No obvious deformity.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow: Normal. No bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight elbow pain, medial epicondylitis.\n\nHypertension.\n\nDiabetes.\n\nPLAN\n\nFor her right elbow pain, I have prescribed a sling for her right arm. I have advised her to apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes 3 times a day. I have prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg, 3 times a day, with food, for 1 week. I have advised her to avoid pottery for the next 2 weeks. She will follow up with me in 2 weeks.\n\nFor her hypertension, I have advised her to continue taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n\nFor her diabetes, I have advised her to increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. I have advised her to check and record her blood sugars daily. I have ordered an A1c. She will follow up with me in 2 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nShe will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Davis is a pleasant 70-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain.\n\nThe patient reports that her pain started 4 days ago. She was working on a pottery wheel and felt a pain on the inside of her elbow. The pain radiates down her arm and along the medial aspect of her arm. She denies any direct injury. She has been taking ibuprofen but denies any relief. She reports a pain level of 7 out of 10.\n\nThe patient reports that she has a history of hypertension and is currently on 10 mg of lisinopril. She reports that her blood pressures have been in the teens to 30s. She reports that she is compliant with her medication regimen.\n\nThe patient also reports a history of diabetes. She is currently on 500 mg of metformin twice a day and reports that she is not compliant with her medication regimen. She reports that her blood sugars have been high. She reports that she likes to bake and eat sweet foods.\n\nShe reports that she enjoys doing ceramics and baking and has been making a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. She denies any prior history of elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient reports that she is also a nature walker.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. No murmurs, gallop, or rub.\n• Cardiovascular: No murmurs, gallop, or rub. No extra heart sounds.\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow shows moderate tenderness to palpation over the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. No cyanosis or clubbing. No skin lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right elbow were obtained and reviewed today. They show no fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nRight elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s x-rays were reviewed today and they show no fracture or bony abnormality. This is consistent with a diagnosis of medial epicondylitis.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed a sling for the patient\'s right arm. I have also recommended that she apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. She can take 600 mg of ibuprofen every 6 hours as needed for pain and inflammation for the next week.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to avoid any heavy lifting or use of the affected limb for the next couple of weeks. I also advised her to continue her nature walks.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s history of hypertension and diabetes were reviewed and discussed today. Her blood pressure was within normal limits today and her diabetes regimen was discussed. She was advised to continue her current regimen and to follow up in a couple of weeks. A referral to a diabetic counselor was also recommended.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: The patient was advised to monitor and record her blood sugar levels daily. She was also advised to follow up with me in a couple of weeks.\n\nRight hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure was within normal limits today.\n\nRight diabetes.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is not compliant with her medication regimen and her blood sugars have been high.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
