, but admits she occasionally forgets to take her medication.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever.\n• Gastrointestinal: Endorses abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.\n• Respiratory: Endorses wheezing, history of asthma.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Mild expiratory wheezes, otherwise normal.\n• Cardiovascular: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n• Gastrointestinal: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium, otherwise soft. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBarbara Johnson is a 31-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of abdominal pain.\n\nAcute gastritis.\n• Medical Reasoning: I believe this is related to her caffeine intake and her moderately heavy alcohol use. She is advised to cut back on alcohol consumption to less than 2-3 drinks per week. She is also advised to reduce her caffeine intake. I will prescribe Zantac 150 mg twice a day.\n• Additional Testing: I will order a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and comprehensive metabolic panel.\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is currently on metformin 500 mg twice daily. I will increase the morning dose to 1000 mg and keep the evening dose at 500 mg.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: Continue to check her blood sugars.\n\nAsthma.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is currently on flovent 100 mcg twice daily and albuterol as needed. She has not had any recent asthma attacks and is advised to continue taking the flovent daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBarbara Garcia is a 31-year-old female with a history of diabetes type 2 and asthma, who presents today with abdominal pain. The patient states she has had this pain for the past 