ms to be doing pretty well continue to take that daily and then also albuterol as needed so how does that sound for a plan any other questions comments suggestions\n[patient] that sounds good and i also feel like i\'m eating been eating a lot of spicy food\n[doctor] hmmm\n[patient] lately that could be contributing towards my gastritis so i\'m gon na cut back on that as well\n[doctor] okay yeah i\'d also recommend while this is hurting you definitely cut back on the spices the spicy food easier said than done sometimes i know but yep yep alright sounds good well listen i\'ll see you back in four weeks give me a holler if if you have any questions or anything else in the meantime\n[patient] i will\n[doctor] alright you take care sure you got\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBarbara Johnson is a 31-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of abdominal pain. The onset of her pain began one week ago. She has a history of diabetes mellitus type 2 and asthma. She notes that she has been consuming a lot of coffee lately and she thinks that may have triggered her symptoms. She describes her pain as moderate in severity and it is located bilaterally in her upper abdomen. The pain is intermittent and is associated with nausea and occasional vomiting. The patient states that she experiences nausea approximately 3 times per week and vomits a couple of times. The pain has been gradually increasing in severity. She notes that her abdominal pain also increases when she exercises. She denies any fever.\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes mellitus type 2. She is currently on metformin 500 mg twice daily. She states her blood sugar levels have been controlled.\n\nThe patient has a history of asthma. She is currently on flovent 100 mcg twice daily and albuterol as needed for wheezing. She states she has not had any recent asthma attacks, but admits she occasionally forgets to take her medication.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever.\n• Gastrointestinal: Endorses abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.\n• Respiratory: Endorses wheezing, history of asthma.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Mild expiratory wheezes, otherwise normal.\n• Cardiovascular: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n• Gastrointestinal: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium, otherwise soft. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBarbara Johnson is a 31-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of abdominal pain.\n\nAcute gastritis.\n• Medical Reasoning: I believe this is related to her caffeine intake and her moderately heavy alcohol use. She is advised to cut back on alcohol consumption to less than 2-3 drinks per week. She is also advised to reduce her caffeine intake. I will prescribe Zantac 150 mg twice a day.\n• Additional Testing: I will order a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and comprehensive metabolic panel.\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is currently on metformin 500 mg twice daily. I will increase the morning dose to 1000 mg and keep the evening dose at 500 mg.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: Continue to check her blood sugars.\n\nAsthma.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is currently on flovent 100 mcg twice daily and albuterol as needed. She has not had any recent asthma attacks and is advised to continue taking the flovent daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBarbara Garcia is a 31-year-old female with a history of diabetes type 2 and asthma, who presents today with abdominal pain. The patient states she has had this pain for the past 1 week, and as you know, she has a longstanding history of diabetes. She believes that her recent coffee intake may have triggered her gastritis. The pain is gradually increasing in severity, and she has associated nausea and vomiting about 3 times in the past week. She has vomited a couple of times. She notes that the pain is also exacerbated with exercise, such as jogging. The patient denies any fever.\n\nShe has been drinking a lot of coffee, and believes that may have contributed to her abdominal pain. She states that she started the coffee last week and towards the end of the week her coffee intake was pretty bad. She has been controlling herself this week and thinks that she probably just had 1 cup. She has not had any recent asthma attacks. She is currently taking Flovent twice a day and albuterol as needed. She states she has not had any asthma attacks lately, so she thinks she could decrease the Flovent, but she thinks that is a good decision. She denies any recent changes in her diabetes regimen. She is currently taking metformin 500 mg once a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever.\n• Respiratory: End expiratory wheezes.\n• Gastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Grade 3 out of 6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n• Cardiovascular: Grade 3 out of 6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n• Gastrointestinal: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium of the abdomen. The abdomen is otherwise soft. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCBC, CMP, and urinalysis within normal limits.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Abdominal pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe the patient has acute gastritis, which I think is related to the caffeine intake and alcohol use.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to cut back on her coffee and alcohol intake. I also advised her to cut back on spicy food. I also advised her to keep her diabetes regimen consistent.\n- Medical Treatment: I prescribed Zantac 150 mg twice a day.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe her diabetes is under control at this time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue to monitor her blood sugar levels.\n- Medical Treatment: I will increase the metformin dose to 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg in the evening.\n\n3. Asthma.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe her asthma is under control at this time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue to take the Flovent daily and use albuterol as needed.\n- Medical Treatment: I will continue her on Flovent and albuterol as needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes type 2, asthma, and gastritis.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of moderately heavy alcohol use. She also enjoys coffee.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily, Flovent twice daily, and albuterol as-needed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. Denies diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Mild end expiratory wheezes, very faint.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium. Abdomen is soft. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Abdominal pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be acute gastritis, likely related to her increased caffeine intake. She has a history of moderately heavy alcohol use as well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed appropriate limitations for alcohol intake as well as spicy foods.\n- Medical Treatment: Reduce caffeine intake. Reduce alcohol intake. Prescription for Zantac twice daily provided. I\'m going to order a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and a comprehensive metabolic panel.\n\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n- Medical Reasoning: She is doing well, but I want to make a slight adjustment to her dosage.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue with home monitoring and bring those readings to her next appointment.\n- Medical Treatment: Metformin 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg at night.\nContinue home blood sugar monitoring.\n\n3. Asthma.\n- Medical Reasoning: She seems to be doing well on her current regimen and has not had any asthma attacks lately.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: She was advised to take Flovent daily despite no recent asthma attacks as this will decrease the frequency of her attacks.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue on daily Flovent. Continue albuterol as-needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
