ve we may have to do we\'re gon na stick with conservative treatment first but we may consider some surgery where i can go in and release that impingement on your nerves through your wrist it\'s a simple outpatient procedure and then i think you would feel better how does that sound\n[patient] that sounds like a good plan i was hoping maybe i could get some relief with some therapy rather than having to go to surgery so i like that plan very much\n[doctor] yeah we\'ll start with the the wrist splint and once i get the nerve conduction studies that will give me another idea whether or not i need to send you some physical therapy in addition or if we need to go directly to surgery but that will all hinge on that nerve conduction test do you so do you have any further questions\n[patient] no that\'s great thank you so much\n[doctor] thank you very much\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is a 45-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand pain. The patient states that she is a data entry clerk and has been experiencing intermittent numbness and tingling in her right hand for approximately 6 months. The patient states that she has been experiencing pain in her right hand for the last month. The pain is mild to moderate and is associated with numbness and tingling in the right hand. The pain is relieved by not typing on the keyboard and using a mouse. The patient states that she has been taking ibuprofen for the pain. The patient denies that the pain wakes her up at night.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right hand pain, numbness, and tingling.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in the left hand. Denies pain in the left hand.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right wrist: Tinel\'s sign positive. Pain with wrist extension and flexion.\nNeurological: Sensation intact in all fingers except the right thumb, index, and middle fingers.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I have examined the patient and discussed her current symptoms with her. I have explained to her that I believe she has carpal tunnel syndrome. I have recommended that she wear a wrist splint at night. I have recommended that she continue to take ibuprofen 400 mg, 1 tablet, 4 times a day. I have recommended that she undergo an EMG of the right wrist. If the EMG is positive, I have recommended that we consider surgical intervention. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand numbness and tingling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAlexis Rodriguez is a pleasant 36-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand numbness and tingling. She reports that she has been sitting at a keyboard all day long and has been experiencing numbness and tingling in her right hand for 6 months. She states that the numbness and tingling have been persistent for the past month. She also reports that she has some pain that she describes as a 6 out of 10. She notes that the pain is worse when she is typing on the keyboard. She notes that rest and ibuprofen seem to help her pain. She also notes that her mouse causes her pain. She denies waking up at night due to the pain or numbness. She denies that her symptoms affect her sleep. She notes that she is currently taking ibuprofen for her pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNEURO\nUpper Extremities\nExamination of the right hand: Flexion pain. Extension pain. Positive Tinel’s sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNerve conduction studies were ordered.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and medical history today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have recommended that she wear a wrist splint at night. I have also