\n[doctor] alright so listen to your lungs so your lungs are clear bilaterally listen to your heart so your heart exam you do still have that grade two out of six systolic ejection murmur but we know it about that before so let me take a look at your elbow so when i press right here on the back of your elbow is that painful\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] alright so when i have you you you flex and extend it it\'s painful does that does that hurt\n[patient] yes it does\n[doctor] alright so your right elbow exam shows you have pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow you do have mild pain with flexion and extension but you do have also normal range of motion at that elbow so we we did do an x-ray before you came in and luckily nothing\'s broken no fracture no bony abnormality so it\'s a normal x-ray which is really good so let me just talk to you a little bit about my assessment and plan for you so you have a elbow contusion i believe your son did hit you with the lacrosse stick and it caused that that pain i see some swelling little inflammation there as well you have a little bit of a bruise i think that\'s that\'s what happened so for that i know you\'re having some pain i\'m gon na prescribe you eight hundred milligrams of ibuprofen you can take that twice a day and that should help with some of that swelling and pain i also want you to ice it three times a day for twenty minutes at a time that should also help with the swelling and pain and just for the time being for the next couple of weeks just you know maybe you know not play lacrosse with your son just to help that heal then you can get back on on the field so for your your high blood pressure we\'re just gon na keep you on the hypertension we\'ll just keep you on that twenty milligrams of lisinopril looks like you\'re doing great with that and your diet and then for your diabetes we will keep you on the five hundred milligrams of metformin and we will also get a a1c just to check your levels and see if we do need to adjust the medication in the future alright so do you have any questions for me\n[patient] no sounds good\n[doctor] alright so we will see you next time and my nurse will be in with those prescriptions\n[patient] alright thank you\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nPhilip Brown is a pleasant 45-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of his pain began approximately one week ago. The patient states he was playing lacrosse with his kids and suddenly started hurting. He denies any specific fall. The patient describes his pain as sharp and localized to the tip of the elbow. The patient states that he tries to take ibuprofen occasionally to alleviate his pain. The patient states that his pain radiates down his forearm. He denies any injury to his elbow, but notes that his child was rough with him while playing lacrosse.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm. No extra heart sounds.\nRESPIRATORY: Lungs are clear bilaterally.\nMSK: Right elbow exam: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension. Normal range of motion.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right elbow were reviewed and are unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nPhilip Brown is a 45-year-old male who presents today with right elbow pain.\nElbow contusion.\n• Medical Reasoning: I believe the patient\'s son hit him with the lacrosse stick and caused the pain. There is some swelling and bruising.\n• Additional Testing: We will get an A1c.\n• Medical Treatment: We will prescribe the patient ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily. We will also recommend ice three times a day for 20 minutes at a time.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: His blood pressure was elevated today. I think he may be having a white coat effect. He is compliant with his diet.\n• Medical Treatment: We will continue him on lisinopril 20 mg.\n\nDiabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: His blood sugar levels are good.\n• Medical Treatment: We will continue him on metformin 500 mg. We will get an A1c to check his levels.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nPhilip Smith is a pleasant 53-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of pain began 1 week ago when he was playing lacrosse with his children. He denies any fall, but notes that his son was checking him several times during the game. He states that the pain is sharp and radiates down his forearm. He has tried taking ibuprofen, which he states has been helpful. He states that he is unable to move his elbow without pain.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension and diabetes. He is currently taking lisinopril 20 mg daily and metformin 500 mg daily. He states that he is compliant with his diet and blood glucose levels typically range between 80 to 100. He denies any chest pain or abdominal pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Gait is within normal limits.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\nCardiovascular: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. No extra heart sounds. No S3 or S4.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension. Normal range of motion.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was performed today and it is unremarkable. No fracture or bony abnormality was identified.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained an elbow contusion when his son checked him during a lacrosse game.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that his x-ray was unremarkable and that he has a right elbow contusion. I advised him that his symptoms are consistent with this diagnosis.\n- Medical Treatment: I have prescribed ibuprofen 800 mg twice a day and ice for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. I advised him to avoid lacrosse until his symptoms have improved.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure today was elevated at 150/90 mmHg.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue taking his lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been compliant with his diet and blood glucose levels typically range between 80 to 100.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue taking metformin 500 mg daily.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg daily. We will obtain a hemoglobin A1c.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension but otherwise normal range of motion.