ifficulty sleeping, states that he is able to ambulate without difficulty, and has been taking an afternoon nap. He denies chest pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nNeck\n• General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nCardiovascular\n• Auscultation of Heart: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\n\nRespiratory\n• Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n• Examination of Lower Extremities: Trace of edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Congestive heart failure.\n• Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue on Bumex 2 mg, oral, daily. Continue to monitor diet and avoid salty foods. Weigh daily and call if weight increases by 3 pounds in 2 days. Referral to a nutritionist for dietary education.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: This was poorly controlled prior to the acute event.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg, oral, daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg, oral, daily. Order renal artery ultrasound to check for secondary causes.\n\n3. Chronic kidney disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: To monitor renal function.\n• Additional Testing: Order blood work.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKevin Smith is an 82-year-old male with a past medical history significant for hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease, who presents for hospital follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been trying to watch his diet, but he admits to having some dietary indiscretions. He notes that he loves to eat pizza and chicken wings, and he does not always take his medications as prescribed. He was recently hospitalized for an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation. He reports that his ankles were swelling and he had shortness of breath. When he arrived at the emergency room, his blood pressure was 200/90. He denies chest pain.\n\nThe patient states that he has been feeling better since he was discharged from the hospital. He reports that he has been watching his diet and taking his medications as prescribed. He notes that he has been urinating a lot since he was in the hospital and that he has been taking a water pill through his IV. He reports that he is on different medications for his blood pressure now. He denies shortness of breath or any problems sleeping. He does report having an afternoon nap. He denies any chest pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Genitourinary: Reports increased urination.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral ankle swelling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Constitutional: In no apparent distress.\n• Neck: In no JVD.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\n• Musculoskeletal: Trace edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved ejection fraction of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Congestive heart failure.\n• Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion in uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to watch his diet and to avoid salty foods. I encouraged him to continue to monitor his blood pressure at home.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue taking his medications as prescribed.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\n3. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Order renal artery ultrasound.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow-up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up after acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports being a football fan.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Bumex 2 mg once daily, Cozaar 100 mg daily, and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral ankle swelling.\nNeurological: Denies any sleep disturbance.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 128/72.\nHR: 79 bpm.\nRR: 26.\nSpO2: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distension noted.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate. There is a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: There is trace edema to the bilateral lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nPrevious echocardiogram is reviewed and revealed a preserved EF of 55% abnormal diastolic filling and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Congestive heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be cause by dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that he should continue to monitor his diet and avoid salty foods. I advised him to weigh himself every day and call me if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg once daily. Referral to nutrition provided for dietary education.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: This was uncontrolled prior to his hospital visit.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diet and home blood pressure monitoring.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg once daily. Renal artery ultrasound ordered for further evaluation.\n\n3. Kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: Due to patient\'s acute CHF exacerbation, this is to be monitored.\n- Medical Treatment: Lab work ordered.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. He is advised to call should he experience weight gain of 3 pounds in 2 days.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


