ates that he was unable to bear weight on his right foot initially but is now able to bear weight with some difficulty. He has been keeping his right foot elevated and icing it. He denies any previous right ankle injuries. He has been taking ibuprofen for pain relief and reports that it is helping. He has been playing a lot of soccer lately and is currently playing for the community league. He notes that his wife and oldest son are also very involved in soccer.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain and swelling.\nNeurological: Denies numbness.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds. Strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Right ankle: No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. No ecchymosis over the medial malleolus. Ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus associated with the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right ankle was obtained and reviewed today. There was no fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight ankle sprain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. We will have him follow up in 2 weeks. He will be advised to keep his leg elevated and ice. He will be given an Aircast for stabilization and crutches for 1-2 days. He may start walking on it as tolerated. He will also be advised to take NSAIDs as needed for pain relief.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Smith is a pleasant 39-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right ankle pain. The onset of his pain started after he fell on the ice yesterday. The patient reports that he was very careful and did not expect to slip and fall. The patient states that he was unable to ambulate on his right ankle initially after the fall. He states that he was assisted by a friend. He is currently able to bear some weight on the right ankle, but is still limping. He has been icing and elevating his right ankle, as well as taking ibuprofen. The patient denies any numbness in his right foot.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he is very active in sports and his favorite sport is soccer. He is currently playing in a community league.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that he has been taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain. Denies right foot numbness.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds in the right foot. Strong dorsalis pedis pulse.\n\nNEURO: Sensation is intact to light touch in the right foot.\n\nMSK: Examination of the right ankle: Ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness to palpation of the foot.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right ankle taken today reveal no fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight ankle sprain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that the patient keep his leg elevated, especially when seated. He will continue to ice his right ankle. He will be given an Aircast to help stabilize the ankle. He will also be given crutches for the next 1-2 days. He may then start walking on it as tolerated. I have also recommended that he take NSAIDs as needed for pain.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain and swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Hernandez is a pleasant 42-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right ankle pain and swelling. The onset of his pain began yesterday after he slipped and fell on the ice while taking out the trash. He states that he was unable to ambulate at the time of injury and that a friend had to help him up. He then proceeded to ice and elevate his ankle throughout the night. His pain has since improved and he is able to bear more weight on his right ankle, however he is still limping. He denies experiencing any numbness to his right foot. In addition to ice and elevation he has been taking ibuprofen for his pain, which has provided some relief. He does not recall any other previous injury to his right ankle.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is very active and enjoys playing soccer. He currently plays for the community soccer league.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals signs look good today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Mild edema\nNEURO: Normal sensation.\nMSK: Examination of the right ankle: Ecchymosis is present. There is swelling over the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. Neurovascular intact. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Sensation is intact to light touch.\n\nRESULTS\n\n3 views of the right ankle were taken today. These reveal no evidence of fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight ankle sprain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. We discussed that his x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture and that he sustained a right ankle sprain. Treatment options were discussed. He was advised to keep his right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. He will also continue to ice his right ankle. An Aircast was provided today to help stabilize his ankle. He is to also utilize crutches for the next 1 to 2 days followed by weight-bearing as tolerated. For pain control he can continue to take NSAIDs as needed. We discussed that he should see significant improvement in his symptoms over the next 2 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to assess his progress and to determine his return to play date. He is advised to call the office should his symptoms change or worsen prior to his next appointment.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


