ates that he was unable to bear weight on his right foot initially but is now able to bear weight with some difficulty. He has been keeping his right foot elevated and icing it. He denies any previous right ankle injuries. He has been taking ibuprofen for pain relief and reports that it is helping. He has been playing a lot of soccer lately and is currently playing for the community league. He notes that his wife and oldest son are also very involved in soccer.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain and swelling.\nNeurological: Denies numbness.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds. Strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Right ankle: No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. No ecchymosis over the medial malleolus. Ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus associated with the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right ankle was obtained and reviewed today. There was no fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight ankle sprain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. We will have him follow up in 2 weeks. He will be advised to keep his leg elevated and ice. He will be given an Aircast for stabilization and crutches for 1-2 days. He may start walking on it as tolerated. He will also be advised to take NSAIDs as needed for pain relief.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Smith is a pleasant 39-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right ankle pain. The onset of his pain started after he fell on the ice yesterday. The patient reports that he was very careful and did not expect to slip and fall. The patient states that he was unable to ambulate on his righ