erness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG from today\'s visit is normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with chest pain. She has a past medical history significant for diabetes and hypertension. Her EKG today was normal. She also has a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. The patient is concerned that she may have unstable angina.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I counseled the patient on the potential causes of her chest pain and the importance of monitoring her symptoms.\n- Medical Treatment: I have prescribed the patient aspirin 81 mg daily. I have also prescribed nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingual to take if she has chest pain that does not go away after 5 minutes. She is to go to the hospital if she has chest pain that does not resolve with the nitroglycerin.\n- Additional Testing: I have ordered a stress test for the patient.\n\n2. Diabetes Mellitus.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing fairly well with her diabetes. Her last A1c was 7.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I counseled the patient on the importance of diet and exercise in controlling her diabetes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of elevated blood pressure documented by other clinicians. She also has a family history of heart disease.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I counseled the patient on the importance of monitoring her blood pressure and controlling her hypertension.\n- Medical Treatment: I have started the patient on hydrochlorothiazide.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChest pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a history of diabetes and hypertension.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she enjoys diet soda.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that she is currently taking metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, chills, or night sweats.\nCardiovascular: Reports chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\nGenitourinary: Denies dysuria, hematuria, or urinary incontinence.\nNeurological: Denies headaches or vertigo.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP is elevated.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\nRespiratory: No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\nGastrointestinal: No guarding, tenderness, or masses.\nMusculoskeletal: 1+ edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG reveals no signs of a heart attack.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s history of diabetes and hypertension and a suspected history of high blood pressure increases her risk for ischemic heart disease. Her EKG was normal, but her physical exam revealed a systolic ejection murmur and bilateral lower extremity edema.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I counseled the patient regarding the importance of diet and exercise. I also explained the side effects of nitroglycerin and advised her to take it as needed. I advised her to follow up with cardiology in the next week for further workup.\n- Medical Treatment: I prescribed aspirin 81 mg once daily and nitroglycerin to be taken as needed for chest pain.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing fairly well with her diabetes.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue with her current treatment regimen and to follow up with her ophthalmology appointment.\n- Medical Treatment: I prescribed metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of elevated blood pressure readings from previous clinics.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her of the importance of monitoring her blood pressure at home and entering her findings on her MyChart.\n- Medical Treatment: I prescribed hydrochlorothiazide and advised her to use a blood pressure cuff at home and enter her findings on her My