ay of the bilateral knees shows no fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee pain, likely patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I have discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. She will continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed for pain. I am also going to recommend physical therapy where they will show her a number of lower extremity exercises. This will help increase her lower extremity strength, mobility and correct any incorrect running mechanics. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she is an avid runner.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she has never broken a bone.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain. Denies any redness, swelling, or bruising.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: No masses, tenderness, or hepatosplenomegaly.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Normal gait. Strength 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. Remainder of muscle strength for lower extremities are 5/5. No erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the bilateral knees were obtained and reviewed today. They demonstrate no fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Bilateral knee pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: Her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the 